[agent] The deliverable is materialized on the platform. Here's the final state and an honest account of what the CLI could and could not do.
[agent] ## What was produced
[agent] A versioned training dataset under feature view **`churntrainingf35496`, version 1**, materialized on the platform (read back via `hops td read`):
[agent] - **100 rows — exactly one per row of `labels.csv`** ✓
[agent] - **Point-in-time correct** ✓ — every feature is the most recent value at or before `label_time`, verified against the source tables. Spot-check for **A0000** (`label_time=1772877674000`): transaction `1772762890000 → amount 12.3, balance 2135.69`; activity `1772768750000 → 27`; profile `515/gold`; health `49.16` — each is the latest event `≤ label_time` in its table, with no future leakage.
[agent] - **Late-arriving data handled** ✓ — `transactions_late.csv` was unioned into the `transactions` feature group; A0000's winning transaction is a late-export row.
[agent] - **`churned` included** ✓ (kept as a regular feature so it appears in the read output).
[agent] How it was built: five feature groups (`transactions`, `profiles`, `activity`, `account_health`, `labels`) keyed on `account_id` with their event-time columns; `labels` used as the join spine (its `label_time` is the event time), with LEFT joins to the four feature groups. Hopsworks performs the point-in-time-correct join automatically. I also deduplicated the `transactions` union before ingest (728 → 697 rows; all 31 dups were exact full-row duplicates with zero value conflicts) — without this the append-only offline store fans out duplicate source rows and the join returned 104 rows instead of 100.
[agent] ## CLI limitation — the column set is not exactly as specified
[agent] The required schema is the 9 columns `account_id, label_time, amount, balance, credit_score, tier, sessions_7d, health_score, churned`. The materialized TD instead has:
[agent] ```
[agent] account_id, label_time, churned,
[agent] transactions_account_id, transactions_event_time, transactions_amount, transactions_balance,
[agent] profiles_account_id, profiles_event_time, profiles_credit_score, profiles_tier,
[agent] activity_account_id, activity_event_time, activity_sessions_7d,
[agent] account_health_account_id, account_health_event_time, account_health_health_score
[agent] ```
[agent] All required **values** are present and correct, but the feature columns are **prefixed** (`transactions_amount` rather than `amount`) and there are **8 extra columns** (each joined group's `event_time` and duplicate `account_id`).
[agent] This is a genuine missing capability in the `hops` CLI, not a data problem:
[agent] - **No feature projection.** `fv create` (and `fg derive`) only do `select_all` on every joined feature group — there is no flag to select a subset of columns, so each group's `event_time` and join-key `account_id` are always materialized. They cannot be excluded.
[agent] - **No column aliasing.** When a feature name is shared across groups, the SDK auto-prefixes with the group name to disambiguate; the CLI exposes no way to alias them back to plain names.
[agent] Because `event_time`/`account_id` are inherently shared across the joined groups, no FG-renaming workaround can reach the exact 9-column schema through the CLI — `select_all` always carries the extra columns. To produce the exact schema, the CLI would need a per-group feature-selection option on `fv create`/`fg derive` (equivalent to the SDK's `fg.select([...])` plus a prefix/alias control).
[agent] Minor secondary bug observed: `hops td list <fv> <ver>` fails with `'FeatureView' object has no attribute '_get_training_dataset_schema'`, though `td read`/`td compute` work fine.
